\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

We scope our claims to the specific architecture and experimental conditions studied. Several limitations bear on the generalizability and completeness of our findings.

\paragraph{Single primary environment.}
Our diagnostic analysis centers on Walker Walk from the DeepMind Control Suite. While this task provides a well-calibrated testbed for detecting value overestimation---dense rewards, clearly distinguishable performance levels, and sufficient complexity to expose instabilities---conclusions drawn from a single environment may not capture failure modes specific to other observation structures, reward landscapes, or action dimensionalities. A Cheetah Run baseline (010a) is running at the time of submission; results are not yet available.

\paragraph{Limited seed count.}
Each key experimental condition is evaluated with two seeds. The $\text{nve}{>}1.5\text{x}$ diagnostic threshold achieves 4/4 threshold separation between trained and untrained $\Delta t$-embedding conditions; across all 8 experimental conditions, overall classification accuracy is 7/8 (AUROC\,=\,0.933), with the single false positive (008e\_v3\_s2) collapsing via a non-CIVO pathway. Stochastic collapse is inherently difficult to characterize with few seeds: observed collapse rates of 1/2 yield wide binomial confidence intervals. For instance, the true collapse probability for trained $\Delta t$-embedding with $\Delta t{=}1$ imagination could plausibly range from 5\% to 95\% given our sample size. We report all seeds transparently rather than selecting favorable outcomes.

\paragraph{Replay sampling seed.}
In experiments prior to the final round, the replay buffer's episode sampling routine used a fixed seed rather than the per-run seed; model weight initialization and all other stochastic components received correct per-run seeds. Because the shared replay ordering is a common-mode factor affecting experimental and control conditions identically, between-group contrasts remain unbiased, though within-group variance may be slightly underestimated. The final seed (seed 3, exp\_008d) uses the corrected, fully independent implementation.

\paragraph{NVE threshold calibrated and evaluated on the same data.}
The $\text{nve}{>}1.5\text{x}$ diagnostic threshold was identified from the same experiments used to evaluate its separating power. No held-out validation set was used. The threshold achieves 4/4 separation for the trained-vs-removed $\Delta t$-embedding comparison, but the overall 7/8 classification accuracy across all 8 conditions yields a 95\% Clopper--Pearson confidence interval of $[47.3\%,\, 99.7\%]$; the supplementary $\hat{r}_k > 1.7$ threshold achieves 8/8 with CI $[63.1\%,\, 100\%]$. Independent validation on additional configurations or environments is needed to confirm the threshold's reliability as a general diagnostic.

\paragraph{Single base algorithm.}
All experiments use DreamerV3 with its RSSM world model. The identified causal chain---trained $\Delta t$-embedding $\to$ conditioning-induced value overestimation $\to$ collapse---is mechanistically tied to the RSSM's latent-dynamics architecture and the imagination-based actor-critic training loop. Whether analogous failure modes arise in other world-model architectures (e.g., transformer-based dynamics models or discrete latent spaces) remains untested. Notably, TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} demonstrates successful $\Delta t$-conditioning in a model-predictive control setting, suggesting the vulnerability may be specific to imagination-based training rather than $\Delta t$-conditioning per se.

\paragraph{No formal causal inference framework.}
Our analysis is interventional---we manipulate experimental factors and observe outcomes---but does not employ a formal causal inference framework (e.g., structural causal models or do-calculus). We use the term ``causal'' loosely to describe controlled ablations that isolate necessary conditions. A more rigorous treatment would strengthen the mechanistic claims.

\paragraph{Pathway B mechanism not fully characterized.}
Experiment 008e\_v3 seed 2 collapsed via a dynamically distinct pathway: eval-driven destabilization with increasing policy entropy, rather than the value-driven exploitation with decreasing entropy seen in classic CIVO (008d seed 1). While we observe this divergence and rule out seed-specific fragility (the matched baseline seed remains stable), the root cause of Pathway B remains an open question. Characterizing this second vulnerability pathway is necessary for developing robust mitigations.

\paragraph{Full stop-gradient results pending.}
Gradient decomposition reveals that 93\% of $\Delta t$-embedding gradients originate from observation decoders, with 0\% from the reward head. The full stop-gradient experiment (018), which blocks the dominant observation-decoder gradient pathway, had not completed at the time of writing. This leaves open whether gradient-level intervention at the correct source suffices to prevent CIVO, or whether the vulnerability persists through the remaining 7\% KL-dynamics pathway.

% Experimental Setup — Draft v1
% Target: CoRL 2026
% Status: Draft, pending Director review

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

We design a controlled factorial experiment to isolate the mechanisms by which variable-rate imagination degrades performance in world-model reinforcement learning. All experiments use DreamerV3 \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3} as the base architecture, with modifications limited to the imagination rollout mechanism and temporal conditioning.

\subsection{Base Architecture and Modifications}
\label{sec:base_arch}

Our base agent is DreamerV3 \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3} with its Recurrent State-Space Model (RSSM) world model. The RSSM learns latent dynamics from experience and generates imagined trajectories for actor-critic learning. In the standard configuration, each imagination step corresponds to a single environment transition ($\Delta t = 1$).

We study a \textit{variable-rate imagination} mechanism that modifies two components of the standard pipeline:

\paragraph{Temporal embedding.} We add a temporal step-size embedding $e_{\Delta t} \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$ that conditions the RSSM transition function on the imagination step size $\Delta t$. We study three configurations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Trained} ($d{=}32$, gradients enabled): the embedding is jointly optimized with the world model, allowing the RSSM to specialize its predictions for different $\Delta t$ values.
    \item \textbf{Untrained} ($d{=}32$, gradients disabled): the embedding is randomly initialized and frozen, providing a fixed but uninformative temporal signal.
    \item \textbf{Removed} ($d{=}0$): no temporal conditioning is provided to the RSSM.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Temporal subsampling.} When enabled (\textsc{subsample on}), the replay buffer stores both $\Delta t{=}1$ and $\Delta t{=}2$ transitions, exposing the world model to multi-rate dynamics during training. When disabled (\textsc{subsample off}), only standard $\Delta t{=}1$ transitions are stored, and $\Delta t{>}1$ is used only during imagination rollouts.

\paragraph{Imagination step size.} During imagination, the agent rolls out trajectories at a fixed step size: either $\Delta t{=}1$ (standard) or $\Delta t{=}2$ (skipping one intermediate step per imagination transition).

\paragraph{Imagination horizon.} The number of imagination steps per rollout: $H{=}8$ or $H{=}15$ (DreamerV3 default). With $\Delta t{=}2$ imagination, an $H$-step rollout covers $2H$ environment steps of lookahead.

All other DreamerV3 components---the actor, critic, reward head, and continuation predictor---remain unmodified.

\subsection{Environment}
\label{sec:environment}

We evaluate on continuous control tasks from the DeepMind Control Suite \citep{tassa2018deepmind}:

\paragraph{Primary environment.} \textbf{Walker Walk}: a planar bipedal locomotion task with 24-dimensional proprioceptive observations (14 orientations, 1 height, 9 velocities) and 6-dimensional continuous actions. This task requires sustained coordination and provides a challenging testbed for diagnosing value overestimation, as the reward signal is dense and performance differences are clearly distinguishable.

\paragraph{Cross-environment validation.} We include \textbf{Cheetah Run} (17D observations, 6D actions) to test whether the identified mechanisms generalize beyond Walker Walk.\footnote{Cheetah Run baseline is in progress at the time of submission (010a, 116K/512K steps).}

All environments use \texttt{action\_repeat=2} and \texttt{time\_limit=1000}, with proprioceptive (non-pixel) observations processed by MLP encoders and decoders.

\subsection{Factorial Experiment Design}
\label{sec:factorial}

The core of our diagnostic methodology is a factorial experiment that systematically varies three factors governing variable-rate imagination. Table~\ref{tab:factorial} summarizes the experimental conditions.

\paragraph{Factors.}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Temporal embedding type}: trained ($d{=}32$) vs.\ untrained ($d{=}32$, frozen) vs.\ removed ($d{=}0$).
    \item \textbf{Subsampling strategy}: \textsc{on} (replay buffer contains both $\Delta t{=}1$ and $\Delta t{=}2$ transitions) vs.\ \textsc{off} ($\Delta t{=}1$ only).
    \item \textbf{Imagination horizon}: $H{=}8$ vs.\ $H{=}15$.
\end{enumerate}

